{
  "dom_id": "step-e241",
  "name": "Example: Comparing the Relative Magnitude of a Trigonometric Function",
  "problem": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\cos x| \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x^2+1$ grows without bound as $x\\to\\infty.$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1} = 0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2+1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951126/assets/b48e8a196c5a5ec3b44ec114dcdd4faf85219578c91552925b61cf1c1a65e17f.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
